menu "Firefly bring-up (S15 slice b)"

    choice FF_BRINGUP_STAGE
        prompt "Display/touch bring-up stage"
        default FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_1
        help
            S15 slice b is bring-up-in-the-loop: the maintainer flashes one
            stage, looks at the glass, then advances. Each stage is a strict
            superset of the previous one's init, so a dark screen at stage N
            still tells you exactly how far the earlier stages got from the
            serial log. Switch with `idf.py menuconfig` (this menu) or set
            CONFIG_FF_BRINGUP_STAGE=N in sdkconfig before building.

        config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_1
            bool "Stage 1 - first light (panel only, no LVGL)"
            help
                IO expander up, release LCD_RST + TP_RST, backlight on,
                SPD2010 QSPI panel init, then esp_lcd_panel_draw_bitmap a
                solid fill and a two-colour split. Proves the expander,
                reset release, QSPI wiring, init sequence, RGB565 byte order
                and orientation with no GUI stack in the way.

        config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_2
            bool "Stage 2 - the app on glass (LVGL v9, one face)"
            help
                Everything in stage 1, then LVGL v9 up via esp_lvgl_port and
                one real ff_shell face rendered (Radar, no selection) — the
                same projection the sim renders for that state.

        config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_3
            bool "Stage 3 - touch drives it (SPD2010 -> shell input)"
            help
                Everything in stage 2, then the SPD2010 touch controller is
                wired to an LVGL pointer indev so a physical tap/swipe feeds
                the SAME abstract input events the sim ctl socket injects,
                changing shell state. On boot the stored touch calibration
                (if any) is loaded from ff_settings and applied.

        config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_4
            bool "Stage 4 - calibrate touch (crosshair capture, then run)"
            help
                Everything in stage 3, then the S15d touch-calibration flow:
                tap five on-screen crosshairs, the per-axis affine is fitted
                (ff_touchcal_solve), the four params + all five raw->screen
                pairs are logged, and the transform is applied LIVE — boot
                continues straight into the normal UI so the corrected touch
                can be felt immediately, no reflash. NOTE: app_main still
                boots against the stub (no-op) store, so the fit is
                session-live only; bake the logged params as a compile-time
                default (or wire an NVS store) to persist across reboot.
    endchoice

    config FF_BRINGUP_STAGE
        int
        default 1 if FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_1
        default 2 if FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_2
        default 3 if FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_3
        default 4 if FF_BRINGUP_STAGE_4

    config FF_COMPASS
        bool "Compass — GY-273 magnetometer + onboard QMI8658 tilt (S15)"
        default y
        help
            Brings up the GY-273 magnetometer wired to the puck's back
            header (docs/hardware/comms-brain.md: SDA/SCL on the same
            pins the touch/expander bus already uses) and the onboard
            QMI8658 accelerometer, tilt-compensates via ff_geo_heading_deg
            (firmware/core), and pushes the result into the shell at
            10 Hz (ff_shell_set_heading) so the Radar arrow can point.

            Auto-detects which magnetometer chip is actually present
            (QMC5883L, HMC5883L, or QMC5883P — see ff_compass.h) at
            boot; a puck with no magnetometer wired up still boots fine
            with this ON — the driver logs "no magnetometer" and
            heading stays the shell's own -1 ("unknown") default
            forever, same honest degrade as every other optional
            sensor in this bring-up.

            Default ON: the GY-273 is part of this project's intended
            hardware (docs/specs/S15-esp32s3-target.md deliverable 3),
            not a bring-up diagnostic to opt into. Turn off only to rule
            out this driver while debugging an unrelated I2C-bus issue.

    config FF_MIC
        bool "Mic — onboard I2S MEMS microphone (S30)"
        default y
        help
            Brings up the onboard I2S MEMS microphone on its own I2S1
            port (BCLK GPIO15, WS GPIO2, DIN GPIO39 — see
            docs/specs/S30-audio-input.md) as an off-by-default HAL:
            ff_mic_init allocates the channel but never starts reading
            until something calls ff_mic_start (the bench console's
            `mic on`, or a future S29/Music face — no product feature
            reads the mic yet, this slice is bring-up only).

            Honest by construction: init failure OR the driver's own
            "all-zero/stuck data for 1 second" sentinel check both read
            as "MIC absent" everywhere this is surfaced (bench console,
            Settings -> DIAGNOSTICS) — a puck with no working mic still
            boots and is fully usable, same degrade every other optional
            sensor in this bring-up already follows (ff_compass, ff_audio).

            Default ON: the mic is part of this board's onboard hardware,
            not a bring-up diagnostic to opt into — mirrors FF_COMPASS's
            own default-on reasoning above. Turn off only to rule this
            driver out while debugging an unrelated I2S-peripheral issue
            (it shares no pins, bus, or peripheral instance with
            ff_audio's own I2S0 speaker path — see ff_mic.h).

    config FF_DEMO_MODE
        bool "Demo mode — boot into the fictional Firefly Fields world"
        default n
        help
            S20 demo mode (docs/specs/S20-demo-mode.md). When enabled, the
            firmware EMBED_FILES the demo festpack
            (firmware/assets/demo/firefly-fields.festpack.json) and, on
            boot, seeds the shell into "Firefly Fields" via ff_demo_seed:
            a fictional festival lineup, five seeded crew members
            (live / close-range / stale / no-fix) and a Signals feed, with
            the wall clock latched to Saturday 21:30 — so every face shows
            real-looking data with NO mesh, comms brain or real festival.

            The seeded state is genuinely computed through the real core
            APIs (honest freshness/close-range/no-fix), but the mode is a
            DEMO: it does not connect to a radio and shows a fixed,
            frozen world. Leave OFF for field builds. Meaningful at
            bring-up STAGE 2 or 3 (a face must render to see it).

    config FF_DEMO_LIVE
        bool "Live demo — advancing clock + synthetic event feed (S23)"
        depends on FF_DEMO_MODE
        default n
        help
            S23 demo-feed (docs/specs/S23-demo-feed.md). Extends S20's
            static snapshot into a LIVE world: after seeding, the demo
            clock ADVANCES from the seeded epoch by real elapsed time (so
            ages tick and scheduled events fire), and the pure-core
            ff_demofeed generator drives a reproducible stream of synthetic
            crew signals and presence pokes into the SAME ff_wiring feed
            path a real mesh uses. Incoming signals accumulate in the
            Signals feed and crew presence drifts LIVE -> STALE -> LOST and
            recovers, so the new faces have something live to build against.

            Requires FF_DEMO_MODE. When OFF (the default), FF_DEMO_MODE
            keeps S20's EXACT frozen-clock static snapshot — no generator,
            no advancing clock. Everything this switch adds is demo content,
            gated with FF_DEMO_MODE and stripped from a field build. LIVE is
            reproducible: a fixed seed + the demo epoch make the same run
            produce the same event stream (goldens use fixed snapshots, not
            wall-clock live generation — S23 AC6).

    config FF_FIELD_PACK
        bool "Embed and load the field festpack at boot (live mode)"
        depends on !FF_DEMO_MODE
        default y
        help
            S05 field festpack (docs/specs/S05-festpack.md). When enabled,
            the firmware EMBED_FILES the real field festpack
            (firmware/assets/field/lost-lands-2026.festpack.json — a
            verbatim copy of fest-almanac's Lost Lands 2026 pack, see that
            dir's README) and, on boot, parses it into the shell via
            ff_shell_load_pack. That is ALL this does: no crew, no
            positions, and no clock are seeded (honest data — CLAUDE.md).
            Loading the pack gives the MAP face a real origin, Settings a
            real UTC offset, and tightens the wall-clock plausibility
            window to the festival's own dates (±14 days) instead of the
            compile-time default. Everything else (roster, positions,
            signals) still comes only from the real mesh.

            Mutually exclusive with CONFIG_FF_DEMO_MODE (a live field
            build never also seeds the fictional demo world) — enforced
            both by this "depends on" and by a compile-time #error in
            app_main.c. Default ON: this is what a live (non-demo) field
            build should do.

    config FF_GLASS_RULER
        bool "Draw the glass-ruler calibration pattern at boot and hold it"
        default n
        help
            Device-only diagnostic for the bezel/pixel-array offset
            investigation (docs/hardware/glass-offset.md): draws a
            boot-time "ruler" pattern (a crosshair + r=205 outer circle
            centered on the panel, four edge rulers with 2px ticks out to
            40px, four 45-degree corner markers, and dx/dy wrap-test
            striped blocks at the left and top edges) directly to the
            panel with esp_lcd_panel_draw_bitmap — no LVGL — right after
            the panel comes up, THEN HOLDS IT FOREVER (a 1s heartbeat log,
            never continuing to the boot splash, LVGL, or the normal UI)
            so the maintainer can read the offset by eye against the
            physical bezel with no time pressure.

            Default OFF: zero behaviour change to every other build. The
            drawing function itself is compiled out entirely
            (#if CONFIG_FF_GLASS_RULER) when this is off, so a field or
            demo build is byte-identical with or without this diagnostic
            existing in the tree — verify with `idf.py size` if in doubt.

    menu "Mesh link (S15c — UART transport to the comms brain)"

        choice FF_LINK
            prompt "Mesh transport"
            default FF_LINK_NONE
            help
                What ff_shell_cfg_t.transport is wired to at boot. NONE
                (today's default, unchanged behaviour) leaves it zeroed —
                ff_shell_init skips mc_connect, link stays
                FF_SHELL_LINK_NONE, and CONFIG_FF_DEMO_MODE's loopback
                sender (if also enabled) still makes sends visible with
                no radio at all. UART wires a real mc_client_t to the
                comms brain over the header exposed on this board (see
                the options below).

                A TCP option deliberately does NOT appear here: TCP is
                the SIM target's dev stand-in for talking to meshtasticd
                (firmware/meshclient/mc_transport_tcp.c) and is excluded
                from the device build entirely — it is POSIX sockets
                code that does not exist under ESP-IDF/newlib (see
                targets/esp32s3/components/ff_meshclient/CMakeLists.txt's
                own comment). Offering a "TCP" choice item here that
                could never actually be selected/built would document
                nothing true about this target.

            config FF_LINK_NONE
                bool "None (no mesh transport)"

            config FF_LINK_UART
                bool "UART to comms brain (Meshtastic Serial Module PROTO)"
        endchoice

        choice FF_UART_PORT
            prompt "UART port for the mesh transport"
            depends on FF_LINK_UART
            default FF_UART_PORT_1
            help
                ESP32-S3's UART peripherals route through the GPIO
                matrix, so EITHER port can be wired to GPIO43/44 via
                uart_set_pin() — this choice is about avoiding a conflict
                elsewhere in this build, not about wiring capability.

                UART1 (default): nothing else in this build touches it,
                so remapping it onto GPIO43/44 costs nothing and needs no
                other config change.

                UART0: NOT free by default, even though GPIO43/44 are
                ALSO UART0's own default pins. This target's committed
                sdkconfig.defaults leaves ESP-IDF's stock console config
                in place — PRIMARY console = UART0, SECONDARY console =
                USB-Serial/JTAG (the default combo; see
                esp-idf's components/esp_system/Kconfig ESP_CONSOLE_UART/
                ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY choices, and this project's own
                app_main.c S26f comment, which states outright that on
                the maintainer's real board "USB-Serial/JTAG is the
                SECONDARY console" — UART0 stays primary). esp_console
                already owns a uart_driver_install() on UART0 in that
                configuration; installing this transport's own driver on
                the same port would either fail outright or interleave
                framed protobuf bytes with plain-text log lines on the
                same wire. Select this ONLY after confirming (idf.py
                menuconfig -> Component config -> ESP System Settings ->
                Channel for console output) that UART0 is not also the
                console on your build.

            config FF_UART_PORT_0
                bool "UART0 (only if console is off it — see help)"

            config FF_UART_PORT_1
                bool "UART1 (default — no console conflict)"
        endchoice

        config FF_UART_PORT
            int
            depends on FF_LINK_UART
            default 0 if FF_UART_PORT_0
            default 1 if FF_UART_PORT_1

        config FF_UART_TXD
            int "UART TXD GPIO (-> comms brain RXD)"
            depends on FF_LINK_UART
            default 43
            help
                Puck UART header pin (Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-1.46:
                TXD/RXD/3V3/5V/GND), per docs/specs/S15-esp32s3-target.md
                deliverable 3. All pins here are Kconfig so a header
                remap needs no code change. The wire crosses: this pin
                goes to the comms brain's RXD.

        config FF_UART_RXD
            int "UART RXD GPIO (<- comms brain TXD)"
            depends on FF_LINK_UART
            default 44

        config FF_UART_BAUD
            int "UART baud rate"
            depends on FF_LINK_UART
            default 115200
            help
                Matches Meshtastic's Serial Module PROTO mode, which runs
                at a fixed 115200 8N1 with no flow control.

        config FF_UART_LOOPBACK_SELFTEST
            bool "Loopback self-test at boot (TXD jumpered to RXD)"
            depends on FF_LINK_UART
            default n
            help
                Bench bring-up aid (S15c): before a comms brain (Seeed
                XIAO ESP32S3 + Wio-SX1262) is available to talk to,
                jumper the header's TXD pin directly to its RXD pin and
                enable this. At boot, writes a known 64-byte pattern
                through the transport and reads it back within 100 ms,
                logging PASS/FAIL with byte counts — proving the driver
                and header pins end to end with no second board needed.

                Leave OFF once a real comms brain is wired up: the jumper
                must be removed first (it would short the puck's own TX
                to its own RX), and this self-test's writes/reads would
                otherwise race the real want_config handshake over the
                same port.

    endmenu

    config FF_DEV_TRUST_CHANNEL
        bool "DEV/FIELD STOPGAP: auto-pair every node heard on the link"
        default n
        help
            DEV/FIELD STOPGAP: auto-pair every node heard on the link (the
            Meshtastic channel is private, so membership is the crew). Off =
            only the explicit pairing UI grows the roster. Never ship on by
            default.

            Mirrors the sim target's `ffsim --dev-trust-all` (S16 AC6) on
            device, through the exact same field and NodeInfo-only
            auto-pair branch in ff_shell.c/.h (see ff_shell_dev_trust_all's
            doc comment) — a bare Position still never pairs. Unlike the
            sim flag, this does NOT suspend the self filter or latch the
            wall clock from a host observation: those exist only to work
            around the sim's single-node dev harness playing every role,
            which does not apply to a real puck talking to a real,
            distinct comms-brain node id.

            Bench/field stopgap for the Sep 18-20 field test while S12 (the
            real Crew/pairing screen) is built in parallel — see
            docs/hardware/comms-brain.md. Logs
            "firefly: DEV_TRUST_CHANNEL on — auto-pairing every heard node"
            once at boot when enabled. With this OFF (the default), device
            builds are byte-for-byte equivalent in roster-trust policy to a
            build that never had this option: the roster only grows via
            ff_shell_pair, called from a user action.

    config FF_AUDIO_SELFTEST
        bool "Play FLARE_SENT once at boot (S27 sounds device bring-up aid)"
        default n
        help
            Device-only bring-up aid for the S27 sounds device HAL
            (docs/specs/S27-sounds.md, ff_audio.h): once LVGL is up and the
            first face is on glass, plays the FLARE_SENT pattern (the
            short rising 3-note chime) exactly once via ff_audio_play, so
            the maintainer can hear the speaker work without actually
            triggering a flare (which would take over the whole UI). Logs
            when it fires.

            Default OFF: zero behaviour change to every other build (no
            unsolicited sound on a normal boot). Enable, flash, listen,
            then disable again before a field build — see this spec's
            device on-glass steps for the full bring-up sequence.

    config FF_DEBUG_CONSOLE
        bool "Bench/debug console over USB-Serial-JTAG (opt-in, bench/test only)"
        default n
        help
            Opt-in bench/debug console: reads line commands off the
            USB-Serial-JTAG port — the SAME port that already carries the
            boot log (this project's committed sdkconfig sets
            CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y) — so an
            end-to-end test can trigger sends, flares, and state dumps
            over the puck's USB port without a human tap on the
            touchscreen. See docs/hardware/comms-brain.md, "Bench
            console", for the full command reference and an example
            session.

            Polled once per render-loop frame, and ONLY while
            usb_serial_jtag_is_connected() reads true (the same S26f
            amendment sample app_main.c already takes every frame to
            decide light-sleep inhibit) — a line command works exactly
            when a bench USB cable is plugged in and does nothing at all
            on battery, so this changes zero behaviour for field/battery
            operation. Every command dispatches through ff_shell_intent
            (the same QUICK_FLARE/FLARE_END intents the physical 5-tap
            gesture and the sender overlay's CANCEL button use) or the
            debug-only ff_shell_debug_send_text seam
            (app/include/ff_shell.h) — never a new roster-growth path,
            never a direct mc_client send bypassing the shell.

            Default OFF: with this unset, ff_debug_console.c's entire
            body is `#if defined(FF_TARGET_SIM) ||
            defined(CONFIG_FF_DEBUG_CONSOLE)` — compiled out entirely,
            not merely disabled at runtime, exactly like
            CONFIG_FF_DEV_TRUST_CHANNEL/CONFIG_FF_GLASS_RULER above — and
            app_main.c's own polling code under this same guard vanishes
            too, so a field build is byte-for-byte the same with or
            without this feature existing in the tree. Enable only for a
            bench/e2e-test build; never ship it on for a field build.

endmenu
